# ~/.agent/agent.env — host-side credential wiring for agent sandboxes.
# Copy to ~/.agent/agent.env. The `sandbox` wrapper resolves it at launch; the
# container receives the variables per-session (remoteEnv), never the file.
#
# PREFERRED: references into a secrets manager — no plaintext at rest. Two ways:
#
# a) 1Password (built in as the worked example — biometric unlock via `op run`):
#   GITHUB_TOKEN=op://Agents/agent-github/token
#   CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=op://Agents/claude-setup-token/credential
# (any op:// value switches the wrapper to `op run` mode; needs the 1Password
# desktop-app CLI integration enabled).
#
# b) Any other manager via AGENT_ENV_COMMAND — a command that prints KEY=value
# lines; the wrapper sources its output into the session (never onto disk).
# Make it the only line in this file, e.g.:
#   AGENT_ENV_COMMAND="sops -d $HOME/.agent/agent.env.enc"          # sops + age
#   AGENT_ENV_COMMAND="infisical export --format=dotenv"            # Infisical
#   AGENT_ENV_COMMAND="doppler secrets download --no-file --format env"
#   AGENT_ENV_COMMAND="bws secret list $PROJ | jq -r '.[]|\"\(.key)=\(.value)\"'"  # Bitwarden SM
#   AGENT_ENV_COMMAND="gopass show -o agents/env"                   # pass/gopass
#
# Plain values below still work as the fallback (chmod 600, plaintext on disk).
#
# THE RULE: prefer scoped service tokens here. Every resolved variable is
# readable by the agent inside the container — that is the intended contract for
# narrow identities. A personal identity works and the boot check warns on it; a
# scoped service account is the recommendation for unattended fleets.

# ── with-atlassian variant (leave empty for the base, no-MCP profile) ──────────
# Prefer a dedicated service account, allowlisted to the spaces/projects the repo's
# tasks need, attributable in audit logs (a personal identity works — the boot check
# warns and recommends a service account for unattended fleets). Scope follows the task class:
# read-only for context-gathering tasks, comment/transition rights where the job is to
# keep Jira tickets updated (that delegation is the point — the identity doing it
# just isn't yours).
ATLASSIAN_URL=
ATLASSIAN_AGENT_EMAIL=
ATLASSIAN_AGENT_TOKEN=

# Identity pattern the boot check accepts (default: svc-/bot-/agent- prefixes).
# AGENT_IDENTITY_PATTERN=^svc-

# ── GitHub (PR delegation + private marketplace) ───────────────────────────────
# Fine-grained PAT ONLY (github_pat_*), on the bot/service account, with
# **Resource owner: your org** — the token then physically cannot read or
# write anything outside the org (kills token-exfil to attacker repos on
# github.com, which the egress allowlist alone can't stop).
# Pragmatic default, one-time setup: All repositories, contents:read+write +
# pull_requests:write, 1-year expiry, stored in the secrets manager, shared by all repos.
# Tighten later (selected repos, shorter expiry) only if a repo's threat model
# demands it. Classic PATs (ghp_*) can't be org-limited — the boot check
# warns on them. The boot check prints which identity these land as.
# GITHUB_USER=
# GITHUB_TOKEN=

# ── LLM vendor auth (all optional — default is attended login into the volume) ─
# Long unattended Claude runs: `claude setup-token` on any machine → 1-year
# inference-only token. Sidesteps headless OAuth refresh, which is unreliable.
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# CI/fleet tier: org-held credentials outrank volume login automatically (Claude
# Code auth precedence) — same image, no template fork. Prefer org-held service
# credentials for fleets; a personal token delivered here (or via the vault) is accepted.
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Unattended repos/CI: make the boot check FAIL (exit 1) on missing vendor auth
# instead of warning. Leave off for attended first-boot (you're about to log in).
# AGENT_REQUIRE_AUTH=1
